'''''{{Lang|de|Liebesträume}}''''' (German, 'Dreams of Love') is a set of three solo [[piano]] nocturnes (S.541/R.211) by [[Franz Liszt]] published in 1850.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://imslp.org/wiki/File:Liszt_-_S541_Liebestraume_(kistner)_mono.pdf|title=Liebesträume, S.541 (Liszt, Franz)|publisher=imslp.org|access-date=8 February 2022}}</ref> Originally the three ''{{Lang|de|Liebesträume}}'' were conceived as [[lied]]er after poems by [[Ludwig Uhland]] and [[Ferdinand Freiligrath]]. In 1850 two versions appeared simultaneously as a set of songs for high voice and piano, and as transcriptions for piano two-hands.<ref name="Liszt's Liebesträume, PianoTV">{{cite web|url=https://www.pianotv.net/2016/08/liszt-liebestraume-musical-journey/|title={{sic|Liebestraume|hide=yes}} by Liszt: A Musical Journey|publisher=pianotv.net|date=27 August 2016|access-date=8 February 2022}}</ref>

The two poems by Uhland and the one by Freiligrath depict three different forms of love.<ref name="Liszt's Liebesträume, PianoTV" /> Uhland's "{{Lang|de|Hohe Liebe|italic=no}}" (exalted love) is saintly or religious love: the "martyr" renounces worldly love and "heaven has opened its gates". The second song "{{Lang|de|Seliger Tod|italic=no}}" (blessed death) is often known by its first line ("{{Lang|de|Gestorben war ich|italic=no}}", "I had died"), and evokes erotic love; ("I was dead from love's bliss; I lay buried in her arms; I was wakened by her kisses; I saw heaven in her eyes"). Freiligrath's poem for the third [[nocturne]] is about unconditional mature love ("Love as long as you can!", "[[O lieb, so lang du lieben kannst]]").
